{"page_content": "2. The maximum daily exposure value is then identified and is used as the surface water concentration (Clocal) in the risk assessment. That is, the maximum concentration in surface water over 365 days is used in the risk assessment.\n\nFor the sediment compartment the equilibrium partitioning method between water and suspended solids (section A.16-2.3.4) is used to calculate Clocal_sed_freshwater from Clocalwater.\n\nA.16-2.4.5 Secondary poisoning and humans exposed indirectly via the environment\n\nThe calculations explained in A.16-2.3.8 and A.16-2.3.9 are applicable also in case of the direct release to agricultural soil other than via sludge, with the differences described below:\n\n- Instead of PECwater, the annual average concentration in surface water is used to estimate fish concentration used for freshwater secondary poisoning and human\u2019s consumption of fish:\n\nClocalwater,annual = sum of C(t) (calculated for each day for 365 days)/365, with C(t) calculated as explained above.\n\n- The indirect exposure of humans will be considered via the standard humans via environment exposure model taking into account the exposure in the various compartments. However, only groundwater is considered as drinking water, not the surface water from the ditch.\n\n- The concentration in groundwater is calculated for both the arable land and the grassland, and the higher value should be used in the assessment.\n\nAppendix R.16-3 Model for Regional assessment\n\nA.16-3.1 Releases at regional level\n\nAll regional releases associated with the different identified uses, both industrial and wide disperse sources, are cumulated to estimate the total regional release (kg/day) to surface water, wastewater, air and soil. The regional releases associated with the different identified uses are based on the tonnage at regional level for each use and the same release factors used at local scale.\n\nBy default, the tonnage at the regional level for the industrial settings (i.e. manufacture, formulation and industrial uses) is set equal to 100% of the tonnage at EU level, while for wide dispersive uses it is set equal to 10% of the registrant\u2019s supply volume at EU level. Releases at the regional scale are assessed for water, air and soil (including industrial soil). At this scale, direct releases to soil are also considered.\n\nThe default regional releases are therefore calculated, for each use, according to the following formula:\n\nE-egional,1U,j = Qregional daily,IU \u00b0 RFiu,; - 1 000\n\nWhere: J\n\n= environmental compartment (air, soil, wastewater)\n\nE;egional,tu,j (kg/day): release rate to the compartment \u201cj\u201d at the regional scale for an identified use (IU);\n\nQregional daily,1u (tonnes/day): average daily use at the regional scale for an identified use (IU) = regional tonnage for each use/365 days;\n\nRegional tonnage for each use (tonnes/year) = 100% x total registrant\u2019s tonnage at EU level (for industrial setting);\n\nRegional tonnage for each use (tonnes/year) = 10% x total registrant\u2019s tonnage at EU level (for widespread uses);\n\nRFiu, ;: Release factor (% or kg/kg) to compartment \u201cj\u201d for identified use. The default value is set by ERCs (see see Appendix A.16-1 and Chapter R.12 of the Guidance on IR&CSA). If the registrant has more information (market data), the volume to be used for the regional calculation could be refined.\n\nAs stated before, when calculating the total regional releases, by default, 80% (representing the EU average) of the wastewater is assumed to be treated in an STP and 20% to go directly to surface water without any treatment, regardless of the assumptions made about STP connection at local scale.\n\nThe formulas to be applied for the calculation of the total regional release to air, surface water, wastewater and soil are the following:\n\nE total, regional, air = \u00bb E-egional,IU,air\n\nE total, regional, soil = \u00bb E-egional,IU, soil\n\nE total, regional, wastewater \u2014 \u00bb E;egional,IU, wastewater x 80/100\n\nE total, regional, surface water \u2014 > E-egional,IU, wastewater x 20/ 100 where:\n\nEtotal,regional,wastewater Passes through an STP and, subsequently, is discharged in surface water.", "metadata": {"doc_id": "ce323527-7cf4-4069-af01-272e47394ea2", "content_type": "CompositeElement", "filename": "IR_CSR_R16_V4_FINAL.pdf"}}